Yes—the distinction you’re making is between consuming an endpoint and inhabiting an arc.

Porn often compresses sexuality into a highly edited reward sequence: cue → escalation → climax. Real intimacy can be much richer because anticipation, uncertainty, affection, teasing, pauses, attention, trust, and emotional meaning are part of the experience rather than disposable steps before the “point.”

That is also why edging works as a broader metaphor: delay can increase salience. The tension is not merely an obstacle to release; the tension is part of the reward. Rush too quickly and you collapse the state space.

I’d just refine the gender claim. Many women do report that context, trust, feeling desired, emotional connection, and anticipation strongly affect arousal, and “responsive desire” is common. But there is huge individual variation, and men can be intensely context-dependent too. Better to learn your partner’s actual arousal architecture rather than substitute a rule about women generally.

So for a marriage:

attention → safety → anticipation → play → escalation → release → affection → lingering connection → anticipation again

Then intimacy stops being an event with a finish line and becomes an ongoing loop.

That is probably the larger thing you’re pointing at: don’t optimize away the journey when the journey is where most of the value lives.